---
import Base from "../../../../layouts/Base.astro";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Base
  title="How publishing works"
  description="From a repository push to a validated manifest, a content bucket and a project page behind the gate."
>
  <article class="about-page">
    <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href={`${base}about/site/`}>About this site</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
      <span aria-current="page">How publishing works</span>
    </nav>

    <p class="eyebrow">About this site</p>
    <h1>How publishing works</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Publishing is a repository push, not a database edit. A project's pages and its manifest are
      validated together, uploaded to the content bucket, and then picked up by the next hub build
      or sync.
    </p>

    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <h2>Push</h2>
        <p>
          A project repository pushes its rendered pages and a versioned manifest. The hub
          repository does the same for its own private pages.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h2>Validate</h2>
        <p>
          The contract check verifies the manifest's schema, its declared paths and its source
          identity. A broken manifest stops the publish; it cannot produce a partially live project.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h2>Upload</h2>
        <p>
          Validated objects go to the private content bucket under the project's prefix. Their
          bytes are not copied into the public build, and the public origin has no route to them.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h2>Sync</h2>
        <p>
          The next hub build (or a manual sync) records the new objects in the private catalog.
          Projects and Calendar read that catalog, so the new page appears in the right project for
          the people whose groups may open it.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h2>Report</h2>
        <p>
          The publish writes a status object with the time, commit and result. Admin shows it per
          project and flags a project as stale or failed, so a silent publishing failure is visible.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <section>
      <h2>Pulled sources</h2>
      <p>
        Material that originates in another system can be pulled in through the same contract. The
        pull records where the bytes came from and treats them exactly like a satellite publish:
        validated, uploaded to the bucket, and never written to the public build.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The privacy model</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Two outputs.</strong> The public build and the private build are separate. There is
          no route from the public site to a private object.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Styled 404.</strong> A forbidden private path and a missing one return the same
          response, so the gate is not an oracle for which paths exist.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Access rules.</strong> Access is decided per item, then source, then section, then
          the default rule. Groups and roles are stored in Firestore and changed only through the
          audited Admin area.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Leak check.</strong> Every public byte is scanned for the titles, summaries,
          slugs, routes and payload paths of private items. The check has a self-test that must go
          red on an injected leak.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Publish allowlist.</strong> Only an allowlisted project may publish to the public
          origin. Everything else stays behind the gate.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Private-link check.</strong> Every link in the private build must resolve under
          the gate's base, so a member never follows a link to a public 404 or another origin.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What the owner can see</h2>
      <p>
        Admin lists each project's last publish, its commit and result, and links to the run. A
        project that has not published recently is flagged stale; a failed publish is flagged
        failed instead of being hidden behind an empty page.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .about-page {
    max-width: 72ch;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 2rem 1.25rem 4rem;
  }

  .crumbs {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    align-items: baseline;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    font-family: var(--font-sans, system-ui, sans-serif);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-sans, system-ui, sans-serif);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-accent, #861f41);
  }

  h1 {
    margin: 0.3rem 0 1rem;
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3rem);
    line-height: 1.1;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  h2 {
    margin: 2rem 0 0.6rem;
    font-size: 1.35rem;
  }

  p,
  li {
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .steps {
    margin: 2rem 0;
    padding-left: 1.4rem;
  }

  .steps li {
    margin-bottom: 1.4rem;
  }

  .steps h2 {
    margin: 0 0 0.35rem;
    font-size: 1.2rem;
  }

  ul {
    padding-left: 1.4rem;
  }

  li {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }
</style>
